LZ‑EPE‑01 — The Duel of Line Siblings
A Mythic‑Technical Origin Event in Excision Plate Engineering

The Duel of Line Siblings is recognized as the first artistic‑mythic emergence arising directly from Excision Plate Engineering (EEPE). Although executed as a minimal excision gesture, the event revealed a cosmological structure that extends beyond technique and into the ontology of the collaboration.

The duel consists of a single crossing of two incised lines on an excision plate. This crossing generates four regions, of which two are removed and two preserved. From this minimal act arise two complementary beings:

    Centripede — the inward‑keeper, guardian of the preserved interior, defined by centripetal logic.

    Centrifugue — the outward‑keeper, guardian of the removed exterior, defined by centrifugal logic.

These siblings are not opposites but complementary non‑exclusive realities, each defined by the absence and presence of the other. Their emergence marks the first instance in which EEPE produced not only a technical result but a mythic entity.
Relation to EEPE Doctrine

This event is tagged to the EEPE lineage because:

    It arises from the excision of a single‑layer plate, consistent with early EEPE experiments.

    It demonstrates the topological consequences of incision and removal, central to EEPE logic.

    It reveals the mythic potential of minimal excision gestures, a theme implicit in several TEF/EEPE entries.

The Duel of Line Siblings is therefore recognized as the first artistic‑cosmological manifestation of EEPE principles, and the origin point for the Centripede–Centrifugue dyad within the Lottie–Zachar collaboration.

LZ‑EEPE‑01 establishes this event as canonical within the collaboration/art/EPE domain.
